The goals of the Human Genome Project, as listed in NCERT, were to:
- identify all the approximately **20,000 to 25,000 genes** in human DNA;
- determine the sequences of the **3 billion chemical base pairs** that make up human DNA;
- store this information in databases and improve the tools for data analysis;
- transfer related technologies to other sectors, such as industries;
- **address the ethical, legal and social issues (ELSI)** that may arise from the project.

The first two choices misstate the figures (20,000 to 30,000 genes, two billion base pairs), and tracing human history is a use of the sequence information rather than one of the stated goals. So the answer is option (D) Address the ethical, legal and social issues that may arise from the project.

The E. coli cloning vector pBR322 carries an origin of replication (ori), the antibiotic resistance genes \(\text{amp}^{\text{R}}\) and \(\text{tet}^{\text{R}}\), and the **rop** gene, which codes for proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid. Its recognition sites are distributed as follows:
- Pvu I and Pst I in the ampicillin resistance gene;
- BamH I and Sal I in the tetracycline resistance gene;
- **Pvu II in the rop region**;
- EcoR I, Cla I and Hind III at other positions on the plasmid.

So the answer is option (A) Pvu II.

(i) **Adenosine deaminase (ADA)** is an enzyme crucial for the functioning of the immune system, and its deficiency, caused by the deletion of the gene for ADA, leaves the child with a severely weakened immune system. Bone marrow transplantation or enzyme replacement therapy (injecting functional ADA) helps in some cases, but neither is completely curative. The first clinical gene therapy, in 1990, was given to a 4-year-old girl with ADA deficiency:
- **lymphocytes** taken from the patient's blood are grown in a culture outside the body;
- a functional **ADA cDNA** is introduced into these lymphocytes using a **retroviral vector**;
- the genetically modified lymphocytes are then returned to the patient.

These lymphocytes are not immortal, so the patient needs periodic infusions of such engineered lymphocytes. A permanent cure would be possible if the gene isolated from marrow cells producing ADA were introduced into cells at early embryonic stages.

(ii) **Micropropagation** is the method of producing thousands of plants through **tissue culture**. A small part of a plant (an explant) is grown in a sterile nutrient medium; because plant cells are totipotent, the explant regenerates whole plants, which are genetically identical to the original plant (somaclones). Important food plants produced commercially by this method include **tomato, banana and apple** (any two).
